# Differential Equations
#
# Ordinary and partial differential equations as engineering tools: when solutions exist, when equilibria are stable, and how heat, waves, and potentials separate into modes.

#>mathematics_diffeq
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!mathematics
#:form #!curriculum
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Differential Equations"
 ~#claim: "Linear systems are stable exactly when every eigenvalue has negative real part, and linearization predicts nonlinear behavior only near hyperbolic equilibria."
 ~#proof: "Boyce and DiPrima and Strogatz state the ODE results with these conditions; Evans gives the heat, wave, and Laplace equations."
 ~#door: "Read existence and uniqueness, then stability, then cool a mug and fit the curve."
 ~#audience: "engineering and science students after calculus and matrices"
 continuity: "Differential Equations | negative real part"
 cold_open: "A linear system settles down when every eigenvalue has negative real part; the hard part is knowing when linear is honest."
}

^"provenance"{
 as_of: "2026-10-02"
 generator: "gemini-3.8-flash-high"
 revised_by: "claude-opus-5.5 writer"
